content:
The 3 test failures — fixes
Fix 1 — packages/audit/src/ledger.test.ts (dashboard spend line). The row is 1000 micros = 0.001 USD, which formatMicros rounds to 0.00 USD. The test intends a visible spend line, so bump the row to 10000 micros:

- ledger.record(outcome({ fetchId: 'f1', price: { unitsMicros: 1000, currency: 'USD' }, rail: 'x402', settlementRef: 's' }));
+ ledger.record(outcome({ fetchId: 'f1', price: { unitsMicros: 10000, currency: 'USD' }, rail: 'x402', settlementRef: 's' }));
→ spend 0.01 USD now matches, tests stay faithful to intent.

Fix 2 — packages/gateway/src/broker.test.ts (identity test). The harness builds opts (with ring + clock) but run receives a literal that drops the ring — so Broker never gets one. Pass opts:

  it('records identity on a fulfilled outcome when a ring is present', async () => {
-   const { ledger, budget, rail } = make({ ring: true });
-   const { outcome } = await run({ rail, budget, ledger, origin: client() });
+   const { ledger, budget, rail, opts } = make({ ring: true });
+   const { outcome } = await run(opts);
Fix 3 — packages/license/src/engine.ts (slugFromUrl) — the genuine source-vs-test mismatch. license.id should be a short identifier, not the full URL (the full href stays on rawSource). Change href → pathname:

 function slugFromUrl(url: string): string {
   try {
-    return new URL(url).href;
+    return new URL(url).pathname;
   } catch {
     return url;
   }
 }
→ license.id = /r/73. (If you'd rather keep the full URL as the id, flip the test instead — your call; I recommend the source change.)

Next task — make the project runnable: scripts/demo.ts
package.json already wires npm run demo → tsx scripts/demo.ts, but scripts/ doesn't exist, so that command is currently broken. This is the natural next step after going green: a runnable end-to-end demo exercising all four terminal outcomes (fulfilled / blocked / payment-required / token-rejected) against the in-memory rail — no server needed.

The prompt to hand an agent (or me):

"Implement metered-web-broker/scripts/demo.ts. Use the Broker (@mwb/gateway) with an InMemoryRail (@mwb/rails), BudgetEngine (@mwb/budget), AuditLedger (@mwb/audit) and a KeyRing (@mwb/identity) and renderDashboard (@mwb/audit), all with frozenClock. Walk through one fulfilled fetch (show the receipt + derived obligations from a published RSL Link header), one blocked fetch (budget ceiling exceeded), one payment-required fetch (settle failure), and one token-rejected fetch (403 + identity). After each, call renderDashboard(ledger.entries) so the terminal decision is visible on the audit surface. Run with tsx and print a short summary line per outcome. Keep it dependency-free and deterministic (no real network, injected origin client)."

